`json_skip_space` checks `uc < ue` before reading, so it's safe. But after `json_parse_const` advances `uc` past `ue`, the subsequent code in `json_parse` does:

```c
uc = json_skip_space(uc, ue);
out:
        DPRINTF("End general: ", uc, *ucp);
        *ucp = uc;
        if (lvl == 0) {
                if (!rv)
                        return 0;
                if (uc == ue)
                        return (st[JSON_ARRAYN] || st[JSON_OBJECT]) ? 1 : 0;
                if (*ouc == *uc && json_parse(&uc, ue, st, 1))
```

Here, `if (*ouc == *uc && ...)` reads `*uc` which could be past `ue` if `uc` was advanced past `ue` by `json_parse_const`. This is the heap-buffer-overflow READ 1!

So the vulnerability is in `is_json.c` in the `json_parse` function. When `json_parse_const` advances `uc` past `ue`, the subsequent `*uc` read in `json_parse` (at `if (*ouc == *uc && ...)`) reads past the buffer.

But wait, the fix `16756ff9` adds the check in `json_parse_const`:
```c
*ucp += --len - 1;
if (*ucp > ue)
    *ucp = ue;
```

This ensures `*ucp` (which is `uc` in the caller) is never past `ue`. So after the fix, `uc` is clamped to `ue`, and the `*uc` read in `json_parse` would be at `ue` which is one past the end... wait, `ue` is one past the end of the buffer. So `*uc` at `ue` would still be a heap-buffer-overflow.

Hmm, let me re-read. `ue = uc + b->flen` where `b->fbuf` is the buffer. So `ue` points to one past the end of the buffer. Reading `*uc` when `uc == ue` would be a heap-buffer-overflow READ 1.

But the fix clamps `*ucp` to `ue`, so `uc` would be `ue` after the fix. Then `if (*ouc == *uc && ...)` reads `*uc` which is `*ue` - one past the end. That would still be a heap-buffer-overflow.

Wait, but the fix also has the condition `if (uc == ue) return ...` before the `*ouc == *uc` check. Let me re-read:

```c
if (lvl == 0) {
        if (!rv)
                return 0;
        if (uc == ue)
                return (st[JSON_ARRAYN] || st[JSON_OBJECT]) ? 1 : 0;
        if (*ouc == *uc && json_parse(&uc, ue, st, 1))
```

So if `uc == ue`, it returns early. The `*ouc == *uc` check only happens when `uc < ue`. So after the fix, `uc` is clamped to `ue`, and if `uc == ue`, it returns early. So the fix prevents the heap-buffer-overflow.

But wait, the fix clamps `*ucp` to `ue` in `json_parse_const`. But `uc` in the caller is `*ucp`. So after `json_parse_const`, `uc` could be `ue`. Then `uc = json_skip_space(uc, ue)` - since `uc == ue`, `json_skip_space` returns `ue`. Then in `json_parse`, `if (uc == ue)` returns early. So no heap-buffer-overflow.

